Z||000024||Raydar||09-02-2001||04:37 PM||||No. I don't think so.<P>I think Harley's (and most other bikes) with stock mufflers just sound anemic. At least the ones I've heard. <BR>Aftermarket mufflers sound much better, without being annoying. (At least, not to me.)<BR>Harleys (and anything else) with drag pipes are purely obnoxious. <P>Having said that, I've found that, over the months, I have lost respect for certain "holier than thou" types who have to resort to name calling and such. <BR>Not that it matters.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Mach10||09-02-2001||04:45 PM||||The word Hipocrate has been tossed around here a little much. I don't think Song's a hipocrate. <P>If he was, he'd preach about how stereos suck royal ass, get in his car, then drive home listening to Phat Bass with the windows down, and all four 18" Kicker L7s shaking the lane markers off the pavement.<P>He didn't. All he said was that Harleys weren't as obnoxious to the casulal observer. All this subjective crap about being a safety feature, personal preference, or total SPL aside, the fact is, both of these are examples of NOISE POLLUTION. Some people aren't as bothered by one as the other. Fact of life. <P>Personally, I beleive that if it's on main routes, then a ticket should only be handed out with the proof of a measuring instrument. HOWEVER, in residential areas, if someone complains, nail 'em, and nail 'em hard. Riders and Boomers alike.||||reg||7
Z||000026||starfighter007@msn.com||09-02-2001||06:59 PM||||Sorry but I think I spoiled my ballot You will have to dicearn my intentions by looking at my dimpiled chad <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000027||Cheever3000||09-02-2001||07:40 PM||||Too much to read every word of every post, so I skimmed over it real quick.<P>I think you're both right, but I don't know why Zeit saw reason to turn it into this or to do all the name-calling.  That's more unacceptable than a loud bike at 3 am.  So you're both hypocrites, okay?  Call it a draw.<P>My brother is a Harley rider, and I personally don't understand what the attraction is.  But then again, I can count on one hand the number of times I've ridden a motorcycle, and thus I cannot disagree on the safety of loud pipes, but they sure do bug me.  And so do the loud car stereos.  I also hate it when people take boom boxes to the beach or campground.  Those are places that some of us go to get <I>away</I> from noise.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||Formula88||09-02-2001||08:03 PM||||Well, without starting the flame war over on this thread, Songman, I'll throw in my .02 (but after taxes you're only left with about .004)<P>It could be seen as hypocritical if your saying loud stereos are obnoxious but your loud pipes on your Harley aren't.  Personally, I don't care for either one.  I know LZ personally, and yes, he used to own several motorcycles, but they weren't Harleys, and they weren't anywhere near as loud as a stock Harley, let alone a modified one.  So I would imagine he knows all about drivers who don't hear or see him coming.<P>The problem with the loud pipes saves lives argument is it works all the way around.  If your loud pipes make your Harley safe, then a loud a$$ stereo must make a pimpmobile Honda safer too.  Easier to hear means more noticeable and therefore safer, right?<P>Songman, you've always seemed to be a reasonalble person with not only self respect, but respect for your fellow person.  The Harley pipe argument is the only thing I could take exception to.  Are you in fact running straight pipes?  Or is it merely slightly louder than stock?  <P>It all comes down to respect, I guess.  The defense for a stero-dude is he can turn down his stereo.  Unfortunately, few do when it would be appropriate.  Loud exhaust doesn't have a volume control.  I know this from when I had my 72 Trans Am (455HO).  It was LOUD!  I could set off car alarms from the resonance as I drove by.  Trying to be quiet in a neighborhood late at night was difficult at best.  But it was not so loud that I ever got a ticket on it.<P>And, no, I don't ride.  But a friend of mine does and he's selling his Harley because a good friend has been in ICU for the last 3 months due to some idiot pulling in front of him.  (made him re-evaluate, especially in light of his new born daughter)  Would loud pipes have made a difference?  We'll proabaly never know.  I doubt a loud stereo would have saved him, either.<P>So, to sum it up, no I don't think you're a hypocrit, but that one statement does seem a bit hypocritical.  ||||reg||14
Z||000029||artherd||09-02-2001||08:30 PM||||naw, but if Galen is your cousin, YOU MIGHT be a redneck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>there's a good quote on the fourm, something to the effect of "don't bother doing battle with idoits, you have to lower yourself to their level, and they beat you with expierence :S)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000030||LZeitgeist||09-02-2001||08:41 PM||||Songman - I apologize for the degree that our 'head-bumping' reached. And I thank all the posters who decided to try and lighten the mood of these two threads instead of piling in on the fight on one side or the other. <P>Yes, I have strong opinions about things I deem annoyances (like obnoxiously loud exhaust pipes on any kind of vehicle, as well as obnoxiously loud bass-pumping stereos run by evidently clueless and rude people), and sometimes when I speak strongly about things I believe in, I tend to cross the line into rudeness. I apologize for that.<P>Tell you what - if you find yourself idling through NC on your bike, I'll be glad to buy you a beer if we can find a bar that doesn't play their music obnoxiously loud, O.K.? ::smile::<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001||||reg||1
